Alright, I’ve just added a few important fixes to *settings.js*: I introduced an `offChange` method to allow deregistering listeners, and I tweaked the numeric input handling to format values nicely. Those changes were necessary because the UI was leaking listeners each time the settings panel was rebuilt, and the raw numbers were looking messy.

Now I need to make sure the UI code actually uses the new `offChange` capability. The `buildSettingsUI` function is responsible for constructing the settings panel each time the user opens it. Looking at the current implementation, the function starts like this:

```js
// ---------- UI ----------
export function buildSettingsUI(container, settings, onClose) {
  container.innerHTML = '';
```

Every time this function is called, it clears the container and then proceeds to create input elements, attaching `settings.onChange` listeners to keep the UI in sync with the underlying values. However, there’s no corresponding cleanup of those listeners when the UI is rebuilt or closed. Because I just added `offChange`, I should now make the UI deregister any previously attached listener before attaching a new one, otherwise the same callback will be added multiple times, causing duplicate updates and memory leaks.

The cleanest way to handle this is to keep a reference to the listener that `buildSettingsUI` registers. I can store that reference in a module‑level variable, say `activeUIListener`. Then, at the start of `buildSettingsUI`, I can check if `activeUIListener` is non‑null and, if so, call `settings.offChange(activeUIListener)` to remove the old listener before proceeding. After that, I’ll set `activeUIListener` to the new listener when I create it later in the function (that part will be handled in a subsequent edit). For now, I just need to introduce the variable and the cleanup logic at the very beginning of the function.

To implement this, I’ll replace the existing two‑line header of the UI builder with a few more lines:

1. Declare `let activeUIListener = null;` at the top of the file, just before the UI builder, so it’s scoped to the module.
2. Inside `buildSettingsUI`, before clearing the container, add a check: `if (activeUIListener) settings.offChange(activeUIListener);`.
3. Keep the existing `container.innerHTML = ''` line after the cleanup.

The exact snippet to replace is the comment line and the function signature plus the first line inside the function:

```js
// ---------- UI ----------
export function buildSettingsUI(container, settings, onClose) {
  container.innerHTML = '';
```

I’ll replace that with:

```js
// ---------- UI ----------
let activeUIListener = null;

export function buildSettingsUI(container, settings, onClose) {
  if (activeUIListener) settings.offChange(activeUIListener);
  container.innerHTML = '';
```

This matches the pattern of the other edits (using `replace_all: false` to target just this block) and ensures the UI will no longer accumulate listeners. I’ll issue the edit now.

